WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.240 --> 00:00:03.620
Something terrible happened here.

00:00:04.820 --> 00:00:08.320
And for decades, people didn’t talk about
it.

00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:10.960
“I was an adult before I ever heard about
it.

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:13.560
It was something that was hidden.”

00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:17.640
“This entire historic community was obliterated.”

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:20.429
“Bodies dumped in rivers bodies dumped in
mass graves.

00:00:20.429 --> 00:00:22.640
It was an absolute massacre.”

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:26.520
This story isn’t one you’ll find in most
history books.

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:33.860
And almost 100 years later, the facts of what
exactly happened that day...are still unraveling.

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:40.760
“So we’re driving in what’s known as
Black Wall Street.

00:00:40.770 --> 00:00:47.530
It’s where one of the nation’s worst episodes
of racial violence took place.”

00:00:47.530 --> 00:00:53.870
In 1921 — a neighborhood in Tulsa, Oklahoma
called the Greenwood District, was a bustling

00:00:53.870 --> 00:00:56.530
community of black-owned businesses.

00:00:56.530 --> 00:01:01.550
Tulsa locals know that period of Greenwood’s
history as a kind of Golden Age.

00:01:01.550 --> 00:01:12.530
“If you can imagine just like an old time
downtown, things like movie theaters, pharmacy's,

00:01:12.530 --> 00:01:15.049
hair salons, and so forth.”

00:01:15.049 --> 00:01:16.950
They called it, “Black Wall Street”.

00:01:16.950 --> 00:01:19.420
“It was a mecca, it was a huge success.”

00:01:19.420 --> 00:01:23.829
But Black Wall Street was also an anomaly.

00:01:23.829 --> 00:01:30.149
It thrived at a time when the KKK was incredibly
active in Oklahoma and the nation had just

00:01:30.149 --> 00:01:35.799
been through the “Red Summer of 1919”
— when white mobs murdered black people

00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:38.729
in dozens of incidents across the US.

00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:47.040
“There needed to be a sort of match or an
igniter tossed on these embers and that event

00:01:47.049 --> 00:01:52.930
was, that trigger event, was an incident that
involved two teenagers: Dick Rowland, 19-year-old

00:01:52.930 --> 00:01:54.859
black boy who shined shoes downtown.

00:01:54.859 --> 00:01:58.479
Sarah Page, 17-year-old white girl who ran
an elevator in a downtown building called

00:01:58.479 --> 00:02:00.179
the Drexel Building.

00:02:00.179 --> 00:02:02.609
He went to the building boarded the elevator.

00:02:02.609 --> 00:02:03.609
Something happened.

00:02:03.609 --> 00:02:04.979
Sarah Page began to scream.

00:02:04.979 --> 00:02:07.799
They both ran out of the elevator.”

00:02:07.799 --> 00:02:13.220
Now we don’t know exactly what happened
in this elevator — but a day later, Rowland

00:02:13.220 --> 00:02:16.330
was arrested and taken to the courthouse.

00:02:16.330 --> 00:02:20.920
The local newspaper ran an article claiming
Rowland had assaulted Page.

00:02:20.920 --> 00:02:26.920
Even though Page refused to press charges, the
article was essentially a call to action

00:02:26.920 --> 00:02:27.920
for whites.

00:02:27.920 --> 00:02:32.330
“A large white mob began to gather on the
lawn of the courthouse.

00:02:32.330 --> 00:02:34.570
Dick Rowland was in jail on the top floor.

00:02:34.570 --> 00:02:38.350
A number of black men, several dozen, marched
down to the courthouse to protect him.

00:02:38.350 --> 00:02:39.350
Some of them armed.

00:02:39.350 --> 00:02:43.110
There was a struggle between one of the black
men in the small group and one of the white

00:02:43.110 --> 00:02:44.200
men in a larger group.

00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:48.060
And things sort of went south from from that
point.”

00:02:48.060 --> 00:02:54.070
Hundreds of white people descended upon Black
Wall Street, armed.

00:02:54.070 --> 00:02:59.840
Black residents withdrew behind the railroad
tracks that marked off the Greenwood district.

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:01.760
Some of them were armed, and fought back.

00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:07.470
But they were outnumbered by the white mob, which
shot their way through.

00:03:07.470 --> 00:03:09.900
The white mob murdered.

00:03:09.900 --> 00:03:12.280
They looted.

00:03:12.280 --> 00:03:15.660
And they set fire to Black Wall Street.

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:22.360
“This was the strategy, if you will, of
how to deal with these successful black communities.

00:03:22.370 --> 00:03:26.460
The effects were disastrous.”

00:03:26.460 --> 00:03:29.150
For two days, the Greenwood district burned.

00:03:29.150 --> 00:03:31.260
Martial law was declared.

00:03:31.260 --> 00:03:32.900
And the National Guard was brought in.

00:03:34.120 --> 00:03:38.840
By the time the massacre ended, Greenwood
was in ruins.

00:03:38.840 --> 00:03:42.840
More than 1200 homes were destroyed, and 35
blocks, burned.

00:03:45.500 --> 00:03:49.190
The exact number of casualties is harder to
pin down.

00:03:49.190 --> 00:03:53.100
Some initially only reported that white people
died.

00:03:53.100 --> 00:03:58.960
Others, reported somewhere between 30 and
100 mostly black casualties.

00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:03.760
But estimates now put that number closer to
300.

00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:09.780
As for those that survived, thousands of them
lived in tents cities in the months that followed...and

00:04:09.780 --> 00:04:14.460
were left to pick up the pieces of rubble
they once called home.

00:04:15.640 --> 00:04:19.120
After the massacre, the cover up started.

00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:25.330
Records went missing from city files — including
the very article that started it all.

00:04:25.330 --> 00:04:30.199
It makes photos from this time all the more
important as part of the historical record.

00:04:30.199 --> 00:04:35.599
But back in 1921, these images served a very
different purpose.

00:04:35.599 --> 00:04:40.039
“So photo postcards like these were widely
distributed after the massacre.

00:04:40.039 --> 00:04:45.460
At the time they were a part of white supremacist
culture, and kept as souvenirs of racially

00:04:45.460 --> 00:04:46.780
charged crimes.”

00:04:46.780 --> 00:04:51.319
Now, they’re preserved to make sure this
part of Tulsa’s history isn’t forgotten

00:04:51.319 --> 00:04:56.180
— and they paint a clear picture of how
much destruction there was that day.

00:04:57.759 --> 00:05:03.860
On the postcards, it’s called “The Tulsa
Race Riot” — a name that, itself, sort

00:05:03.860 --> 00:05:05.409
of erases what really happened.

00:05:05.409 --> 00:05:11.840
“By calling it a riot, &nbsp;it's a way of trying
to rewrite the history, assuming that there

00:05:11.840 --> 00:05:14.800
were both sides at fault and that was not
the case.

00:05:14.800 --> 00:05:18.840
I call it a massacre and I call it that, because that’s what
it was.”

00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:22.879
Greenwood eventually rebuilt.

00:05:22.879 --> 00:05:28.449
But nearly a century later, there’s a part
of this story that still haunts the city.

00:05:28.449 --> 00:05:31.870
No one actually knows where the victims’
bodies are.

00:05:31.870 --> 00:05:36.490
“We’ve got to find our people we've got
to put them at rest.

00:05:36.490 --> 00:05:42.870
If not we continue to be haunted by what was
done so many years ago.”

00:05:42.870 --> 00:05:48.849
Kavin Ross, a local writer, is one of many
in Tulsa, descended from people who lost everything

00:05:48.849 --> 00:05:50.029
in the massacre.

00:05:50.029 --> 00:05:56.699
“So in this cemetery there are only two
official victims of the Tulsa race massacre.

00:05:56.700 --> 00:05:58.740
How many victims do you think there are?”

00:06:00.310 --> 00:06:06.680
“After all these years, I think 300 is putting
it mild.”

00:06:06.689 --> 00:06:13.360
In 1997, the city finally put together a commission
to study the massacre, and help piece together

00:06:13.360 --> 00:06:15.500
what happened in 1921.

00:06:15.500 --> 00:06:18.590
They compiled records, and eyewitness accounts.

00:06:18.590 --> 00:06:22.020
“The bullets were just raining down over
us.”

00:06:22.020 --> 00:06:26.379
“They set our house on fire and went right
straight to the curtains and set the curtains

00:06:26.379 --> 00:06:27.870
on fire.”

00:06:27.870 --> 00:06:33.949
These accounts are especially important now
— because none of these survivors are alive

00:06:33.949 --> 00:06:34.949
anymore.

00:06:34.949 --> 00:06:38.080
And they also provided new information.

00:06:38.080 --> 00:06:42.910
Some mentioned trucks - like this one - loaded
with victims of the riot.

00:06:42.910 --> 00:06:46.500
One riot witness in particular came forth, testifying that he saw

00:06:46.500 --> 00:06:49.229
bodies being dumped in Oaklawn Cemetery.

00:06:49.229 --> 00:06:51.629
“This is it.

00:06:51.629 --> 00:06:53.760
This is the area.”

00:06:53.760 --> 00:06:58.820
Using the survivor accounts, records, and
eventually, radar — &nbsp;the city was able

00:06:58.820 --> 00:07:02.800
to pinpoint three locations with anomalies
in the soil.

00:07:04.560 --> 00:07:08.080
Only one step was left, to excavate.

00:07:08.080 --> 00:07:13.120
But it was something the city, at the time,
wasn’t up for doing.

00:07:13.139 --> 00:07:19.349
For many Tulsans, it was a part of history
best forgotten, and not worth investigating.

00:07:19.349 --> 00:07:21.939
In some ways, today, that sentiment remains.

00:07:21.940 --> 00:07:27.460
“Kind of a waste of money." "Why do you think that?" "It’s over. It's done with."

00:07:27.960 --> 00:07:32.900
But there are clear signs of a city that’s
ready to come to terms with a dark chapter

00:07:32.900 --> 00:07:33.990
in its history.

00:07:33.990 --> 00:07:37.440
“Honestly that’s a lot of missing people,
people that probably had families.”

00:07:37.440 --> 00:07:42.289
“We owe it to the people who whose blood
has actually fertilized the grounds of this

00:07:42.289 --> 00:07:42.940
place.”

00:07:42.940 --> 00:07:45.699
“There was a tremendous amount of racism”

00:07:45.700 --> 00:07:48.809
“Injustice plus time does not equal justice.”

00:07:50.060 --> 00:07:53.559
Today, a new mayor is re-opening the investigation.

00:07:53.559 --> 00:08:00.409
“I think a pretty basic compact that a city
makes with its citizens is, if somebody murders

00:08:00.409 --> 00:08:04.379
you we will do everything we can to find out
what happened to you and give your family

00:08:04.380 --> 00:08:05.360
closure.

00:08:05.360 --> 00:08:10.999
Whether you were murdered yesterday or you
were murdered 98 years ago.”

00:08:10.999 --> 00:08:15.949
The city will be looking into the three areas
that the commission noted.

00:08:15.949 --> 00:08:21.339
That process — of &nbsp;finding out what lies
beneath Tulsa — and DNA matching any remains

00:08:21.339 --> 00:08:24.300
with descendants, could take years.

00:08:30.110 --> 00:08:34.220
The investigation is just one part of a bigger
historical reckoning.

00:08:34.220 --> 00:08:42.919
But the reality is, it can't undo the crimes or the cover up
of the 1921 Tulsa Race Massacre.

00:08:44.660 --> 00:08:50.620
“This story is the greatest conspiracy of
silence that I've ever seen in history.”

00:08:55.780 --> 00:08:56.920
“Thanks for watching.

